    "body": "### Describe the bug\n\nSince 3.3.0 the module-init code emits a length index for the string table:\n\n```c\nconst struct { const unsigned int length: N; } bytes_length_index[] = {...};\n```\n\nwith `N = max(index).bit_length()` (`Cython/Compiler/Code.py`, string-table\ngeneration; still unguarded on master `b6fe508`).\n\nIf every bytes constant in a module has length 0 — i.e. the module's only\nbytes literal is `b\"\"` — then `max(index)` is `0`, `(0).bit_length()` is `0`,\nand Cython emits `const unsigned int length: 0;`. A *named* zero-width\nbit-field is a constraint violation in C (C11 §6.7.2.1: a zero-width\nbit-field \"shall have no declarator\"), so the C compiler rejects the module:\n\n```text\nempty_bytes.c:2517:39: error: named bit-field 'length' has zero width\n 2517 |     const struct { const unsigned int length: 0; } bytes_length_index[] = {{0}};\n      |                                       ^       ~\n```\n\nThe existing `if not index: continue` guard covers \"no bytes constants at\nall\", but not \"bytes constants that are all empty\". gcc rejects the same\nconstruct, so manylinux builds fail as well.\n\n### Code to reproduce the behaviour:\n\n```python\n# empty_bytes.py — the ONLY bytes constant in the module is empty\nEMPTY = b\"\"\n```\n\n```bash\ncython -3 empty_bytes.py\ncc -c -I\"$(python -c 'import sysconfig; print(sysconfig.get_paths()[\"include\"])')\" empty_bytes.c\n# -> error: named bit-field 'length' has zero width\n```\n\nNotes on minimality:\n\n- The module must contain no other bytes constant. With the default\n  `binding=True`, adding a `def` makes Cython emit an internal bytes constant\n  (`__pyx_kp_b_iso88591_…`), which raises the max length and hides the bug;\n  a module-level `b\"\"` alone triggers it, and so does any module compiled with\n  `binding=False` whose only bytes literal is `b\"\"`.\n- Found in a real project: one module's only bytes literal was\n  `data = spec.data or b\"\"`, and the whole wheel build (253 extensions) failed\n  on that single module after the build backend resolved 3.3.0.\n\n[reproduce.sh](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/31826843/reproduce.sh)\n\n[empty_bytes.py](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/31826847/empty_bytes.py)\n\n### Expected behaviour\n\nThe generated C should compile. Clamping the width to at least one bit, e.g.\n`max(1, max(index).bit_length())`, or skipping the index when the maximum\nlength is 0, would fix it.\n\n### OS\n\nmacOS 26.6.2 (arm64), Apple clang 21.0.0. Also reproduces on Linux/gcc (manylinux_2_28 container) since it is a C-language constraint, not a compiler quirk.\n\n### Python version\n\n3.12.13\n\n### Cython version\n\n3.3.0 (reproduces). 3.2.9 does not emit `bytes_length_index` at all and compiles the same module fine. Also reproduced with master `b6fe508` (2026-09-03, built from git): it emits the identical `length: 0` line.\n\n### Additional context\n\n\nDownstream workarounds until a fix lands: pin the build requirement to\n`Cython<3.3`, or make sure no module's bytes-constant table is all-empty\n(e.g. `bytes()` instead of `b\"\"`). The second one is fragile because the\ntable also contains Cython-generated bytes constants that are invisible from\nthe source, so whether a given `b\"\"` triggers the bug depends on directives\nsuch as `binding`.",
